<DocsPage id="vector-agent">

          <p>
            The main session can inspect the repository, edit files, run commands, use repository tools,
            and report a reviewable result. Use Plan mode when you want analysis without edits, and
            switch back to an editing mode when the plan is approved.
          </p>
          <p>
            When a big task splits into pieces that do not depend on each other, the agent hands them to subagents
            that run at the same time; a small task it does itself. It calls a subagent specialist when part of the
            work fits one. See
            <a href="/docs/subagents">Subagents and subagent specialists</a>.
          </p>

          <h2 id="modes">Modes</h2>
          <div class="definition-grid">
            <article><strong>Normal</strong><p>The default balance of speed, context, and execution depth.</p></article>
            <article><strong>Quick</strong><p>Answers simple questions with minimal context and fewer tokens.</p></article>
            <article><strong>Fast</strong><p>Around 1.5× the speed, with priority capacity and wider parallel execution, at a higher token cost.</p></article>
            <article><strong>Plan</strong><p>Reviews and proposes an approach without changing repository files.</p></article>
          </div>

          <h2 id="tools">The agent's tools</h2>
          <p>
            The agent changes your repository through a fixed set of first-class tools rather than free-form shell commands, so its behaviour is the same in the desktop app, in the terminal and in GitHub Actions.
          </p>
          <ul class="feature-list">
            <li><strong>Files</strong><span><code>read</code> with line offsets and limits, <code>write</code>, <code>edit</code> for an exact string replacement, and <code>apply_patch</code> for a patch that spans several files.</span></li>
            <li><strong>Search</strong><span><code>glob</code> for filename patterns and <code>grep</code> for content, so it finds code without reading whole directories.</span></li>
            <li><strong>Language servers</strong><span><code>lsp</code> asks your repository's language servers for a definition, the references to a symbol, hover type information or a workspace symbol search — the same answers your editor gets.</span></li>
            <li><strong>The web</strong><span><code>webfetch</code> returns one URL as markdown, text or HTML. In the desktop app the agent searches the web with Vector's own browser: <code>browser</code> opens a Google results page for its words and reads it, the way the address bar turns typed words into a search, after you approve the site. Deep research searches the same way in each research agent's own isolated browser, and falls back once to DuckDuckGo's HTML page when Google asks for a check; Vector never answers a check. There is no search service or search key to set up.</span></li>
            <li><strong>The shell</strong><span><code>bash</code> runs commands in the repository, behind the <a href="/docs/permissions">permission rules</a>.</span></li>
            <li><strong>Keeping it legible</strong><span><code>todowrite</code> maintains the checklist you can see, and <code>question</code> lets the agent stop and ask you a structured question instead of guessing.</span></li>
          </ul>
          <p class="note">
            Tools are built in. <a href="/docs/mcp-and-plugins">MCP servers and plugins</a> add more beside them; they never replace these.
          </p>

          <h2 id="context">Context and compaction</h2>
          <p>
            A long session does not fail when it reaches the model's context limit. Vector compacts it: older turns are summarised and pruned while a protected window of recent work is kept intact, and the run continues from the compacted history. A dedicated internal agent writes that summary, so it is written for the model that has to keep working rather than for a reader.
          </p>
          <p>
            Compaction can also be triggered deliberately, and the transcript records where it happened.
          </p>
          <p>The <code>compaction</code> key in <a href="/docs/configuration"><code>vector.json</code></a> changes when and how it happens:</p>
          <dl class="doc-rows">
            <div><dt>auto</dt><dd>Compact on its own as the conversation nears the model's context (default <code>true</code>). With <code>false</code>, a request that no longer fits ends with the provider's error instead. Setting <code>VECTOR_DISABLE_AUTOCOMPACT</code> does the same.</dd></div>
            <div><dt>max_context</dt><dd>Compact once the conversation reaches this many tokens, even when the model's window is larger, since every request sends the whole conversation again. By default Vector compacts just below a model's long-context price tier, and just below 200,000 tokens on a model with a window of 500,000 or more and no tier. <code>0</code> uses the model's whole window, and any other value below 64,000 counts as 64,000.</dd></div>
            <div><dt>tail_turns</dt><dd>How many of your most recent requests, with the work that answered them, are kept word for word (default 2). <code>preserve_recent_tokens</code> caps how many tokens that kept part may take; by default it is a quarter of the usable context, from 2,000 to 8,000 tokens.</dd></div>
            <div><dt>reserved</dt><dd>Tokens kept free so that compaction itself fits. Setting it without <code>max_context</code> also turns the default ceiling off.</dd></div>
            <div><dt>prune</dt><dd>When you send a prompt after the provider's prompt cache has expired, clear the output of older tool calls, since the provider writes the whole history to its cache again then anyway (default <code>true</code>). Setting <code>VECTOR_DISABLE_PRUNE</code> turns it off.</dd></div>
            <div><dt>clear</dt><dd>During a long run, clear older tool output once the conversation passes 60,000 tokens and at least 40,000 tokens of it can go (default <code>false</code>).</dd></div>
          </dl>
          <pre><code>{`{
  "compaction": {
    "max_context": 120000,
    "tail_turns": 3
  }
}`}</code></pre>

          <h2 id="repository-rules">Repository rules</h2>
          <p>
            A rule is a standard written the way you would say it to a new teammate — "we don't put database query
            logic in the controller". Repository rules live in <code>.vector/RULES.md</code>, a plain Markdown file in
            the repository: write a rule there, commit the file, and your whole team gets the same rules. Rules for
            every repository go in the <code>AGENTS.md</code> in Vector's own config folder, which every session reads.
            The desktop app keeps that folder with its app data:
            <code>~/Library/Application Support/ai.vector.app/config/vector/AGENTS.md</code> on macOS,
            <code>%APPDATA%\ai.vector.app\config\vector\AGENTS.md</code> on Windows and
            <code>~/.config/ai.vector.app/config/vector/AGENTS.md</code> on Linux. The terminal reads
            <code>~/.config/vector/AGENTS.md</code> (<code>%USERPROFILE%\.config\vector\AGENTS.md</code> on Windows)
            instead, so each has its own file.
          </p>
          <ol class="steps">
            <li><span>1</span><div><strong>Write it in plain English</strong><p>No rule language, no schema. One sentence describing the standard.</p></div></li>
            <li><span>2</span><div><strong>Scope it in words</strong><p>Say which files it governs, such as <code>src/app/web/*.tsx</code>, in the sentence itself.</p></div></li>
            <li><span>3</span><div><strong>Commit it</strong><p>The file travels with the clone, so everyone working in the repository reads the same rules.</p></div></li>
          </ol>
          <p>
            In <a href="/docs/vectorscope">Vectorscope</a>, <strong>Don't flag this again</strong> on a finding adds a
            rule to a section Vector manages in the same file, and <strong>Undo</strong> takes it out again. Each line
            in that section is one rule: delete a line to remove the rule, or edit it to change the rule, and Vector
            keeps your change the next time it saves. Vector rewrites everything else in the section, so keep notes of
            your own outside its markers.
          </p>
          <ul class="feature-list">
            <li><strong>Vector agents</strong><span>Read the rules file directly — the engine loads it as an instruction file on every prompt.</span></li>
            <li><strong>Claude Code, Codex, Cursor</strong><span>Read their own instruction files, so Vector copies the rules in its section of the file, as the file has them now, into the prompt it hands them, and tells them to read the rest of the file.</span></li>
            <li><strong>Scope survives as words</strong><span>Instruction files load whole, so the file paths a rule covers are stated in the sentence the agent reads.</span></li>
          </ul>
          <p class="note">
            A rule that conflicts with the task you asked for is reported rather than silently resolved. Vector tells
            the agent to say so instead of quietly picking a side.
          </p>

          <h2 id="instructions">Instruction files</h2>
          <p>
            Vector's agent also gets your instruction files with its instructions: the <code>AGENTS.md</code> in
            Vector's own config folder, or <code>~/.claude/CLAUDE.md</code> when that folder has none, and the
            repository's <code>AGENTS.md</code> files from the folder you work in up to the repository's root, or its
            <code>CLAUDE.md</code> files when there is no <code>AGENTS.md</code> on that path. When the agent reads a
            file further down, an <code>AGENTS.md</code> or <code>CLAUDE.md</code> in a folder between that file and the
            folder you work in comes with it. A repository's instruction file is read only where it really is inside the
            repository, never through a link that leads outside it. Setting <code>VECTOR_DISABLE_CLAUDE_CODE_PROMPT</code>
            leaves the <code>CLAUDE.md</code> files out.
          </p>
          <p>The <code>instructions</code> key in <a href="/docs/configuration"><code>vector.json</code></a> adds more. Each entry is one of these:</p>
          <ul class="feature-list">
            <li><strong>A relative path or pattern</strong><span>Such as <code>docs/conventions/*.md</code>, looked for in the folder you work in and each folder above it up to the repository's root. Every match is included.</span></li>
            <li><strong>An absolute path</strong><span>Or one starting with <code>~/</code> for your home folder, naming a file anywhere on your computer. Its last part may be a pattern.</span></li>
            <li><strong>A web address</strong><span>Starting with <code>http://</code> or <code>https://</code>. Vector fetches it with a five-second limit and at most 1 MiB, and leaves it out when the fetch fails.</span></li>
          </ul>
          <pre><code>{`{
  "instructions": [
    "CONTRIBUTING.md",
    "docs/conventions/*.md",
    "~/notes/house-style.md",
    "https://intranet.example.com/agents.md"
  ]
}`}</code></pre>
          <p>
            Entries from every settings file add up. A repository's own <code>instructions</code> apply only once you
            trust it.
          </p>

          <h2 id="formatters">Formatters and language servers</h2>
          <p>
            Both are off until you turn them on in <a href="/docs/configuration"><code>vector.json</code></a>, and both start only in a repository you
            trust, even when the setting is in your own settings, because they run the repository's own tools and code.
          </p>
          <p>
            With <code>{`"formatter": true`}</code>, Vector formats each file the agent writes, edits or patches, with
            every built-in formatter for that file type whose tool it finds, such as <code>gofmt</code> on your
            <code>PATH</code> for Go, or prettier when the repository's <code>package.json</code> lists it. An object
            turns the built-ins on with your changes: a formatter's name with <code>{`"disabled": true`}</code> turns it
            off, and a new name with a <code>command</code> and <code>extensions</code> adds your own, where
            <code>$FILE</code> stands for the file's path. <code>environment</code> and <code>cwd</code> set its
            variables and working folder.
          </p>
          <pre><code>{`{
  "formatter": {
    "prettier": { "disabled": true },
    "sql": {
      "command": ["my-sql-formatter", "--in-place", "$FILE"],
      "extensions": [".sql"]
    }
  }
}`}</code></pre>
          <p>
            With <code>{`"lsp": true`}</code>, Vector starts the built-in language server for a file type when the agent
            first reads or changes a file of that type, and after each write, edit or patch it gives the agent the errors
            the server reports for that file, so the agent can fix them. In the desktop app, the agent's
            <code>lsp</code> tool asks the same servers. An object turns the built-ins on with your
            changes: a server's name, such as <code>typescript</code>, <code>eslint</code>, <code>pyright</code>,
            <code>gopls</code> or <code>rust</code>, with <code>{`"disabled": true`}</code> turns it off, and an entry
            with a <code>command</code> replaces how a built-in starts or adds a server of your own, which also needs
            <code>extensions</code>. <code>env</code> sets its variables, and <code>initialization</code> is sent to the
            server when it starts.
          </p>
          <pre><code>{`{
  "lsp": {
    "eslint": { "disabled": true },
    "my-dsl": {
      "command": ["my-dsl-language-server", "--stdio"],
      "extensions": [".dsl"]
    }
  }
}`}</code></pre>
</DocsPage>
